---
// THE CALCULATOR RESULT, restructured: a hero that is always visible, and four collapsible
// sections beneath it. This is the DETAIL behind the breadth reveal's GLANCE, and the two are
// wired to be recognisably the same content: the reveal's "fees nobody quotes" row is the
// section here that carries the same name, now costed.
//
// WHAT CHANGED AND WHAT DID NOT. Every element id the calculator's render() writes to is
// preserved exactly, in the same shape, with the same classes. Only the containers around
// them are new. That is deliberate and it is the whole safety strategy for this phase: the
// estimator's script is the most delicate code on the site, so this restructure does not
// touch a line of it. render() keeps calling getElementById and keeps finding everything,
// including the elements it reaches for without a null check. A DOM test drives every control
// afterwards to prove it.
//
// WHY IT NEEDED RESTRUCTURING. The old result was one long column: three numbers, a bar, then
// five stacked groups, then fine print, roughly two thousand pixels of it on a phone. The
// numbers a traveler actually holds were at the top and everything else competed with them
// equally. Now the three named numbers, the shape bar and the plain-English assumptions line
// are the only things that are always open, and the rest is there when it is wanted.
//
// WHAT IS OPEN BY DEFAULT, AND WHY ONLY THAT. "Fees nobody quotes you" is highlighted and
// open; the other three are closed. That is the one section that exists because the rest of
// the internet hides it, so hiding it here behind a click would be a small betrayal of the
// premise. Everything else is detail a reader can ask for.
//
// THE SOURCED LINES (shortlist A) come from calc-lines.js, which reads existing verified
// fields and computes nothing. Each is placed where its unit makes sense:
//   Getting there   entry charges (one-off PER PERSON), named zeros, the departure-tax trap,
//                   and the insurance tier. Everything that happens before you land.
//   On the ground   the season tier beside the hotel line, tips scope, the service-charge
//                   guard. Everything that happens once you are there.
//   Fees            the two card fees with their unit, the lodging tax, the VAT-refund
//                   position, and what a different card would avoid.
// The brief listed the named zeros under both "Getting there" and the fees section. They are
// rendered once, in Getting there, beside the charges they are the counterpart to: showing
// the same $0 twice on one page would read as two separate zeros.
import { calcLinesFor } from '../data/calc-lines.js';
import { avoidableFor, exposureFor } from '../data/avoidable.js';
import { comparisonsFor } from '../data/region-comparisons.js';
import { countries } from '../data/index.js';

const { country: c, tipCalc } = Astro.props;
const L = calcLinesFor(c);
// The avoidable half of the fees section. Per country, and empty items are simply absent
// rather than shown as "not applicable": see avoidable.js.
const avoidable = avoidableFor(c);
// THE EXPOSURE SECTION. The same items, now split into the two sides that head them: what
// this traveler pays if they do nothing, and what they simply never lose once they know.
// The split is declared per item in avoidable.js rather than inferred here, so the number at
// the top and the row underneath it can only ever come from one decision.
const exPay = avoidable.filter(a => a.side !== 'dodge');
const exDodge = avoidable.filter(a => a.side === 'dodge');
// The static half of the two totals. The live half (the fee lines, the card base a
// percentage band applies to, the traveler count a per-person figure multiplies by) is
// filled by the calculator's render() from the data attributes below, because those depend
// on inputs this component cannot see at build time.
const EX = exposureFor(c);
// Whether there is anything honest to measure at all. A country can reach this section with
// items that carry no sourced figure between them, and when it does the section shows the
// list and no total. It never shows a reassuring badge instead: "low exposure" is a claim
// about facts we do not hold, and it is the danger score wearing the other hat.
const exHasFigure = EX.payLive || EX.payPctHi > 0 || EX.payFlatHi > 0 || EX.payPpHi > 0
  || EX.dodgeFlatHi > 0 || EX.dodgePpHi > 0;
// The two groups in the drop-down, in the same order as the two figures above them, so a
// reader who has just read a number finds its rows in the place the number implied. Within
// each group the items keep the reading order avoidable.js sorts them into, which puts the
// price-against-price rows first: a real price beside a fake one is the sharpest thing on
// the list. An empty group does not render, so a country with nothing on one side shows one
// group rather than an empty heading.
const exGroups = [
  { key: 'pay', label: "What you'll pay unless you act", cls: 'cs-half-pay', items: exPay },
  { key: 'dodge', label: "What we'll help you dodge", cls: 'cs-half-keep', items: exDodge },
].filter(g => g.items.length > 0);
// Whether Getting there holds a real entry charge, so the fees half can cross-reference it
// instead of printing the same charge twice.
const hasEntryCharge = !!(L.entryCharges && L.entryCharges.length);
// The region-compare link carries NO figures, on purpose: a number on a link is a claim the
// reader cannot check until they have clicked it.
const cmp = (comparisonsFor(c.slug, countries) || [])[0] || null;
---
<div class="hn-result">

  {/* ================= HERO, always visible ================= */}

  <!-- The three numbers a traveler actually holds, named. On the ground is the hero: it is
       what you spend once you land, the figure this site exists to get right. Getting there
       sits beside it honestly (empty by default, and it says so rather than showing a bare
       $0 that reads as free travel), and Total trip is simply the two added up. No new math:
       these are the same figures render() already computes for the bar and the lines below. -->
  <div class="hn-three">
    <div class="hn3">
      <span class="hn3-l">Getting there</span>
      <span class="hn3-v num" id="hnAir">$0</span>
      <span class="hn3-n" id="hnAirNote">&nbsp;</span>
    </div>
    <div class="hn3 hn3-hero">
      <span class="hn3-l">On the ground</span>
      <span class="hn3-v num" id="hnGround">$0</span>
      <span class="hn3-n">what {c.name} costs once you land</span>
    </div>
    <div class="hn3">
      <span class="hn3-l">Total trip</span>
      <span class="hn3-v num" id="hnTotal">$0</span>
      <span class="hn3-n" id="hnTripNote">&nbsp;</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="erange">Total trip realistically somewhere between <span class="num" id="hnLo">$0</span> and <span class="num" id="hnHi">$0</span>.</p>
  <p class="escope" id="hnSub">&nbsp;</p>

  <!-- The shape of the trip: one stacked bar showing how the total splits across the
       components. Pure CSS widths driven by render(), no chart library. The bar is decorative
       (the sections below carry the real figures), so it is aria-hidden; the legend carries
       each component's share. -->
  <div class="hn-shape">
    <div class="hs-bar" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="hs-seg flt" id="hsFlt"></div>
      <div class="hs-seg room" id="hsRoom"></div>
      <div class="hs-seg spend" id="hsSpend"></div>
      <div class="hs-seg cfee" id="hsCfee"></div>
      <div class="hs-seg afee" id="hsAfee"></div>
      <div class="hs-seg tax" id="hsTax"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hs-legend">
      <span class="hs-l flt" id="hlFlt" hidden><span class="mdot"></span>flights <b>0%</b></span>
      <span class="hs-l room" id="hlRoom"><span class="mdot"></span>hotel <b>0%</b></span>
      <span class="hs-l spend" id="hlSpend"><span class="mdot"></span>on the ground <b>0%</b></span>
      <span class="hs-l cfee" id="hlCfee"><span class="mdot"></span>card fee <b>0%</b></span>
      <span class="hs-l afee" id="hlAfee"><span class="mdot"></span>cash fees <b>0%</b></span>
      <span class="hs-l tax" id="hlTax"><span class="mdot"></span>tourist tax <b>0%</b></span>
    </div>
  </div>

  {cmp && (
    <a class="cs-cmp" href={cmp.href}>See how {c.name} compares across {cmp.region}
      <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg>
    </a>
  )}

  {/* ================= COLLAPSIBLE DETAIL ================= */}

  <div class="cs-set">

    {/* ----- Getting there. Everything before you land. Collapsed. ----- */}
    <details class="cs">
      <summary class="cs-sum">
        <span class="cs-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M17.8 19.8 16 14l-6 2 1.5 4.5L10 22l-2-3.5L4 17l1.5-1.5L10 17l2-6-5.8-1.8a2 2 0 0 1 .4-3.6l1.6-.4 3.3 3.3 4.6-1.4a2 2 0 0 1 2.3 2.8z"/></svg></span>
        <span class="cs-t">Getting there</span>
        <span class="cs-hint">flights, and what the border costs</span>
        {/* The preview is the FLIGHT total only, not flights plus the border charge. The
            brief asked for a combined figure, and it cannot be built honestly: entry charges
            are published as prose with ranges, conditions and their own currencies ("about
            $36 on the official portal, or $30 on arrival", "NZD 17 in the app or NZD 23
            online"). Summing that into one dollar figure would mean picking an end of each
            range and inventing a conversion. So the number here is the one render() already
            computes, and the border charges sit inside as sourced lines with their units.
            Flagged to MAIN. */}
        <span class="cs-prev num" id="prevGetting">&nbsp;</span>
        <svg class="cs-chev" width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"/></svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="cs-body">
        <div class="hn-rows">
          <div class="hn-line" id="hnFlightLine" hidden><span class="hn-k">Flights (<span id="hnFlightTravLabel">2</span> travelers, round trip)<em id="hnFlightNote">paid in dollars at booking, so no foreign fee on this line</em></span><span class="hn-v num" id="hnFlightV">$0</span></div>
        </div>

        {/* A1. ONE-OFF PER PERSON, and it says so on every line: this figure multiplies by
            travelers and never by nights, which is the whole reason it is not in the tax
            engine. The value is the guide's own prose, quoted, so ranges and conditions
            survive rather than being flattened into a single number. */}
        {L.entryCharges && L.entryCharges.map(e => (
          <div class="cs-line cs-line-charge">
            <span class="cs-line-h">{e.label}</span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">one-off, per person</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{e.value}</span>
            {e.source && <a class="cs-src" href={e.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener">official source</a>}
          </div>
        ))}

        {L.entryConditional && L.entryConditional.map(e => (
          <div class="cs-line">
            <span class="cs-line-h">{e.label}</span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">only on some trips</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{e.value}</span>
          </div>
        ))}

        {L.entryRequirements && L.entryRequirements.map(e => (
          <div class="cs-line">
            <span class="cs-line-h">{e.label}</span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">a condition of entry, not a charge</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{e.value}</span>
          </div>
        ))}

        {L.entryExemptions && L.entryExemptions.map(e => (
          <div class="cs-line cs-line-zero">
            <span class="cs-line-h">{e.label}</span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">a way to pay nothing</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{e.value}</span>
          </div>
        ))}

        {/* A proposed fee is a watch-flag ZERO. Pricing it would charge every reader for a
            fee that is not being collected. */}
        {L.entryProposed && L.entryProposed.map(e => (
          <div class="cs-line cs-line-zero">
            <span class="cs-line-h">{e.label}</span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">announced, not charged</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{e.value}</span>
          </div>
        ))}

        {/* A3. A NAMED zero, never a bare $0: the words are what make it a fact rather than
            an empty field. */}
        {L.namedZeros && L.namedZeros.map(z => (
          <div class="cs-line cs-line-zero">
            <span class="cs-line-h">{z.label}<span class="cs-zero">$0</span></span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{z.value}</span>
            {z.source && <a class="cs-src" href={z.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener">official source</a>}
          </div>
        ))}

        {L.schengen && (
          <div class="cs-line cs-line-zero">
            <span class="cs-line-h">The Schengen border<span class="cs-zero">$0</span></span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{L.schengen.ees} {L.schengen.etias}</span>
            <a class="cs-src cs-src-in" href="/passports-and-visas">How the Schengen border works now</a>
          </div>
        )}

        {/* Departure tax is a TRAP, not a line. It is inside the airfare, so giving it a
            figure would double-count it against the fare the traveler entered above. A
            reassuring sentence is the honest treatment. */}
        <p class="cs-note">Departure tax is not a separate line here. Where a country charges one it is collected inside your airfare, so it is already in the flight figure, and adding it again would count it twice.</p>

        {/* A2. A named tier that moves no total. The premium depends on age, cover and trip
            length, none of which this page asks for, so no figure is invented. */}
        {L.insurance && (
          <div class="cs-line">
            <span class="cs-line-h">Travel insurance<span class="cs-tier">{L.insurance.label}</span></span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">not priced here</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{L.insurance.note} A premium depends on your age, the cover and the length of the trip, so this calculator names the priority rather than guessing a figure.</span>
            <a class="cs-src cs-src-in" href="#insurance">What cover is worth in {c.name}</a>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </details>

    {/* ----- On the ground detail. Collapsed. ----- */}
    <details class="cs">
      <summary class="cs-sum">
        <span class="cs-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 21h18"/><path d="M5 21V8l7-5 7 5v13"/><path d="M10 21v-6h4v6"/></svg></span>
        <span class="cs-t">On the ground detail</span>
        <span class="cs-hint">your room, your days, and how much of it is cash</span>
        <span class="cs-prev num" id="prevGround">&nbsp;</span>
        <svg class="cs-chev" width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"/></svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="cs-body">
        <div class="hn-rows">
          <div class="hn-line" id="hnHotelLine"><span class="hn-k">Hotel (<span id="hnNightsLabel">7</span> nights)<em id="hnHotelNote">on the card, prepaid or paid at the desk</em></span><span class="hn-v num" id="hnRoomV">$0</span></div>
          <div class="hn-line"><span class="hn-k">On-the-ground spend<em>food, local transport, activities, tips</em></span><span class="hn-v num" id="hnSpend">$0</span></div>
          <!-- Tips: named as the local norm, never as a computed dollar figure. The figure
               shown is the desk's own restaurant-scoped number from tipping.js, so it is
               scoped to sit-down meals and not applied to spend it does not cover (street
               food, a tuk-tuk, a convenience store). Advisory by design: it adds nothing to
               the total, because the daily spend line above already counts tips. -->
          <div class="hn-line hn-tip" id="hnTipLine">
            <span class="hn-k">{tipCalc.label}<em>{tipCalc.tier && <b>{tipCalc.tier}. </b>}{tipCalc.note} <a href={'/' + c.slug + '/tipping'}>How tipping works here</a></em></span>
            <span class="hn-v hn-tipv">{tipCalc.figure}</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* A5. Categorical, beside the hotel line. seasons.js holds no figures on purpose,
            because a single number for what a month costs is not a sourceable fact. So this
            names the windows and says plainly that the room price is NOT season-adjusted. */}
        {L.season && (
          <div class="cs-line">
            <span class="cs-line-h">When you go</span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">not applied to the room price above</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">
              {L.season.peak && <Fragment>Busiest and dearest: {L.season.peak}. </Fragment>}
              {L.season.cheapest && <Fragment>Quieter and cheaper: {L.season.cheapest}. </Fragment>}
              The room price you entered is taken as given, so nothing here moves the total. We publish no seasonal uplift because an honest one does not exist.
            </span>
            <a class="cs-src cs-src-in" href="#when-to-go">{c.name} month by month</a>
          </div>
        )}

        {/* A6 scope, and A8 the double-tipping guard. Both categorical, and neither adds a
            cent to any total. */}
        {L.tips && (
          <div class="cs-line">
            <span class="cs-line-h">Tipping{L.tips.tier && <span class="cs-tier">{L.tips.tier}</span>}</span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">already inside the daily figure, adds nothing</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">
              {L.tips.none
                ? 'Not a tipping culture, so there is genuinely nothing to add.'
                : (L.tips.scope ? ('Sit-down meals run about ' + L.tips.scope + ' where tipping applies, which is the scope of this norm rather than a rate on everything you spend. ') : '')}
              {L.tips.serviceCharge && <Fragment>{L.tips.serviceCharge}, so read the bill before you add anything on top.</Fragment>}
            </span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="esplit">
          <div class="ebar" aria-hidden="true"><div class="c1" id="cashBar" style="width:40%"></div><div class="c2" id="cardBar" style="width:60%"></div></div>
          <div class="elegend"><span class="el cash"><span class="mdot"></span>cash <b id="cashUsd">$0</b></span><span class="el card"><span class="mdot"></span>card <b id="cardUsd">$0</b></span></div>
          <p class="ehint" style="margin-top:8px">Whether at an ATM or a card terminal in a shop or restaurant, always choose to be charged in the local currency, not dollars. Paying in dollars (called dynamic currency conversion) lets the machine set its own, worse, exchange rate on top of any fee.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    {/* ----- Fees nobody quotes you. Closed like the rest, but still edged. -----
        It used to be the only section open, on the argument that hiding it would betray the
        premise. The number in the header does that job better: a traveler seeing $234 against
        a $4,000 trip opens it themselves, and one seeing $65 against $11,000 has learned
        something true without opening anything. The open state was doing the work the figure
        should have been doing.
        It keeps a subtle left edge because it is still the section this site exists for, and
        it is the same "fees nobody quotes" row the breadth reveal previews further up, now
        costed against the traveler's own inputs. */}
    <details class="cs cs-fees">
      <summary class="cs-sum">
        <span class="cs-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="19" x2="5" y1="5" y2="19"/><circle cx="6.5" cy="6.5" r="2.5"/><circle cx="17.5" cy="17.5" r="2.5"/></svg></span>
        <span class="cs-t">Fees nobody quotes you</span>
        <span class="cs-hint">the part that lands on the statement weeks later</span>
        {/* The unavoidable half only. The avoidable items below carry their own figures and
            are deliberately NOT added in: a total of money you will not spend is not a
            quantity, and folding it into a cost line would be nonsense. */}
        <span class="cs-prev num" id="prevFees">&nbsp;</span>
        <svg class="cs-chev" width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"/></svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="cs-body">

        {/* ================= (a) WHAT YOU'LL PAY ================= */}
        <p class="cs-half cs-half-pay">What you'll pay</p>
        <div class="hn-rows">
          <div class="hn-line hn-fee" id="hnCardFeeLine"><span class="hn-k">Card fee, about <span id="hnFeeQ">3%</span> on the <b id="hnCardBase">$0</b> you put on the card<em id="hnCardFeeNote">your room and the card half of your spend, both on the statement weeks later</em></span><span class="hn-v num" id="hnCardFee">$0</span></div>
          <div class="hn-line hn-fee" id="hnAtmFeeLine"><span class="hn-k">ATM and bank fees on the <b id="hnCashAmt">$0</b> cash you pull<em id="hnAtmNote">a few dollars per withdrawal, plus the roughly 3% most US banks add on foreign cash</em></span><span class="hn-v num" id="hnAtmFee">$0</span></div>
          <div class="hn-line" id="hnTaxLine"><span class="hn-k"><span id="hnTaxLabel">Tourist tax</span><em>collected at the desk even on a prepaid room, per night rather than per trip</em></span><span class="hn-v num" id="hnTax">$0</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="hn-note" id="hnTaxNote" hidden></p>
        {/* The entry charge is a cost too, but it lives in Getting there beside the flights it
            belongs with, and it is one-off per person rather than per night. Cross-referenced
            rather than repeated: the same charge printed in two sections reads as two charges. */}
        {hasEntryCharge && <p class="cs-xref">Plus the one-off entry charge, which sits in Getting there above with the flights, because it is per person rather than per night.</p>}

        {/* THE AVOIDABLE HALF USED TO SIT HERE, and it now has its own section below, because
            it was never the same kind of thing as the three lines above it. Those are costs.
            Those are the ones that are not, and burying them at the bottom of a closed
            section made them read as a footnote to the fees rather than as the point.
            Cross-referenced rather than repeated: the items live in exactly one place. */}
        <p class="cs-xref">What you can refuse, and what the lookalike sites charge, is set out under Costs nobody tells you about below, with the two figures kept apart from these ones.</p>

        {/* A7. Availability, and the real detail only where the guide carries it as a dated,
            sourced field. Where the refund lives in prose, this points at the prose rather
            than lifting a rate out of it, because a figure parsed from a sentence was never
            verified as a figure. Never an estimated dollar refund: that depends on what the
            traveler buys, which this page does not ask. */}
        {L.vatRefund && L.vatRefund.kind === 'sourced' && (
          <div class="cs-line cs-line-back">
            <span class="cs-line-h">{L.vatRefund.label}</span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">money back, not a cost</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{L.vatRefund.value}</span>
            {L.vatRefund.source && <a class="cs-src" href={L.vatRefund.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener">official source</a>}
          </div>
        )}
        {L.vatRefund && L.vatRefund.kind === 'pointer' && (
          <div class="cs-line cs-line-back">
            <span class="cs-line-h">Tax back on shopping</span>
            <span class="cs-line-u">money back, not a cost</span>
            <span class="cs-line-d">{c.name}'s position on tax and refunds is set out in full further up this guide, with the rates and any minimum spend. Nothing is estimated into the total here.</span>
            <a class="cs-src cs-src-in" href="#taxes-and-refunds">{L.vatRefund.heading || 'Taxes and refunds'}</a>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </details>

    {/* ================= COSTS NOBODY TELLS YOU ABOUT =================
        The section this whole page is for, given an anchor and a measure of its own.

        WHY IT IS NOT A DANGER SCORE, which is the thing it would most easily have become.
        Everything below is AVOIDABLE. A single blended number over avoidable costs would be
        a fear gauge, and a fear gauge is structurally the same move as the lookalike site's
        countdown timer: it works on the reader rather than for them. It would also be
        arithmetically wrong, because it would add money the traveler will hand over to money
        they will never lose, and those two are not the same quantity.

        So there are TWO numbers and they never touch. Amber is what this trip costs if the
        reader does nothing at all. Green is what stays in their account the moment they know,
        and green is the one that matters: it is the value of the advice, quantified, which is
        the opposite of a threat.

        THE BAR IS A PROPORTION, NEVER A SCORE. Its full width is the trip total the
        calculator already computes, so the two blocks can only ever show how small this is
        against the trip. A bar that cannot exceed the real total cannot inflate.

        WHERE A FIGURE IS A RANGE THE BAR SHOWS THE RANGE: a solid block to the low end, a
        hatched block out to the high one. Collapsing a sourced band to its midpoint would
        invent the one number nobody published. */}
    {avoidable.length > 0 && (
      <section class="ex" id="costs-nobody-tells-you-about" aria-labelledby="exHead">
        <div class="ex-top">
          <span class="ex-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3 3.6 6.4v5.1c0 4.6 3.6 8.4 8.4 9.5 4.8-1.1 8.4-4.9 8.4-9.5V6.4z"/><path d="M9.4 12.1 11.3 14l3.5-3.6"/></svg></span>
